WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.958 --> 00:00:05.292
Built in 607AD in Nara, Japan

00:00:05.375 --> 00:00:09.833
Horyu-ji Temple is the world's oldest
surviving wooden building.

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:12.833
Five storeys high,
it's still standing firm

00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:17.625
despite being made from cypress trees
felled over 1,400 years ago.

00:00:18.292 --> 00:00:21.792
And Horyu's great age
reveals a message for our future -

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:25.833
building with timber can be durable,
beautiful and practical

00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:28.458
and it can even help
tackle climate change.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.667
Here's why.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.875
If we made 90% of our new buildings
from wood

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.833
rather than concrete and steel

00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:39.375
we could reduce
our global carbon emissions by 4%.

00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:42.792
That's more than the total
climate footprint of flying.

00:00:43.458 --> 00:00:46.417
So why does building with wood
do such good?

00:00:46.708 --> 00:00:51.042
First, trees absorb carbon dioxide
from the air as they grow.

00:00:51.125 --> 00:00:54.458
This is naturally stored
in the beams and panels of buildings,

00:00:54.542 --> 00:00:57.375
a process known as carbon capture.

00:00:57.458 --> 00:00:59.625
So the timber in Horyu Temple

00:00:59.708 --> 00:01:03.000
contains CO2 absorbed
from the planet's atmosphere

00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:04.792
in the 7th Century.

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:08.208
Of course,
using wood means felling trees,

00:01:08.292 --> 00:01:10.833
but so long as you're not chopping
down ancient forests,

00:01:10.917 --> 00:01:13.750
you're maintaining the wildlife,
replanting,

00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:18.125
and putting wood to long-term use,
it's sustainable.

00:01:18.542 --> 00:01:21.208
In the last 15 years,
in the northern Hemisphere

00:01:21.292 --> 00:01:27.375
total forest cover has increased
by an area of around 242,000 sq km,

00:01:27.458 --> 00:01:29.917
about the size of the United Kingdom.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.875
In fact, every seven seconds the
sustainable woodlands of Europe

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.958
yield enough timber to build
a four-person family home.

00:01:38.042 --> 00:01:39.708
And it's not just Europe -

00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:44.333
Canada can harvest enough trees every
year to house about a billion people.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.042
An equally important carbon reduction
comes from what you're not using -

00:01:49.125 --> 00:01:53.333
construction with natural materials
demands far less concrete and steel

00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:56.542
and these are
massive climate culprits.

00:01:57.125 --> 00:02:02.083
Steel-making accounts for around
8% of total global CO2 emissions

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:04.792
and cement accounts for a further 6%.

00:02:04.875 --> 00:02:07.417
OK, not all of this
is going into buildings,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.208
but they are a big customer.

00:02:10.125 --> 00:02:14.208
It's the combination of stored carbon
and avoided emissions

00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:17.750
that really tips the climate scales
for timber,

00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:22.208
and a recent innovation has made wood
much more user-friendly.

00:02:22.292 --> 00:02:24.875
CLT, or cross-laminated timber,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.042
is made by layering conifer planks
at 90 degrees to each other

00:02:29.125 --> 00:02:32.292
in three to seven tiers
and then gluing them together

00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:34.875
producing a kind of chunky plywood.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.417
These panels are suitable for walls,
floors, roofs, even lift shafts.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.375
and their strength-to-weight ratio
is often better than concrete.

00:02:44.458 --> 00:02:48.000
CLT can be used in schools,
offices and warehouses

00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:50.125
and now there's a race to the sky

00:02:50.208 --> 00:02:53.750
with 85m 'plyscrapers'
recently completed

00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:55.625
in Austria and Norway.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:59.083
So why are wooden buildings
still the exception?

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:02.208
Mainstream construction
is a risk-averse industry

00:03:02.292 --> 00:03:04.625
and changing this
takes radical action.

00:03:05.042 --> 00:03:08.042
One approach is law -
the French government has decreed

00:03:08.125 --> 00:03:12.833
that at least 50% of every new public
building must be made of wood.

00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:16.292
This not only allows those
structures to store more carbon

00:03:16.375 --> 00:03:18.875
but also encourages
a generation of builders

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.750
to learn a timber-friendly skillset.

00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:24.250
But how safe are wooden buildings,
you may ask?

00:03:24.333 --> 00:03:25.750
Remembering the Great Fire

00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:29.833
that gutted the medieval centre
of London in 1666?

00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:32.917
In fact,
tests of CLT panels in a blaze

00:03:33.042 --> 00:03:35.833
have shown they
tend to form a charcoal layer

00:03:35.917 --> 00:03:37.750
that protects the core

00:03:37.833 --> 00:03:41.167
and when combined with
heat-resistant glues and claddings

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:45.417
they can match or exceed the fire
resilience of conventional buildings.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.917
There are other benefits too.

00:03:47.042 --> 00:03:48.917
Studies suggest that wooden schools

00:03:49.042 --> 00:03:51.792
can actually
lower the heart rate of pupils.

00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:55.583
Even looking at a wooden wall
can reduce blood pressure.

00:03:56.042 --> 00:03:59.583
So wood is not only renewable,
safe and climate-friendly,

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:01.417
it can also make you feel good.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.000
Or to put it another way,
using wood is really good.

